 "articoli": {
  "a13": {
   "titolo": "Stroq – a local audit of what your coding agent did and why",
   "link": "https://github.com/AGGIB/Stroq",
   "data": "2026-11-05T15:54:41+00:00",
   "testo": "Stroq reads your own Claude Code and Codex sessions and shows how many commands came from text your agent read, and which keys left your machine. An example: a made-up machine with made-up sessions, keys and hosts. Hoo is Stroq's owl. Your card shows your own sessions. npx @stroq/cli It reads your last 30 days of sessions, your key files and the folders your agent worked in; prints key names, never values; writes nothing but a small cache under ~/.stroq; and sends nothing. No account, no telemetry. npx @stroq/cli a card of your own sessions, like the one above npx @stroq/cli init stop it next time: guard your agent npx @stroq/cli why why an action was stopped npx @stroq/cli attack watch Stroq stop 36 known attacks, in a sandbox npx @stroq/cli uninstall remove Stroq Every moment on the card has a stroq show line that prints the evidence behind it (npx @stroq/cli show 1). stroq help --all lists the other commands. Needs Node.js 22 or newer. Apache-2.0. Stroq is a local security platform for AI coding agents: Claude Code, Cursor, Codex CLI and others. Its hooks judge every tool call before it runs. They deny curl | sh and outbound calls that carry a known secret from your machine, and they ask you first when a command repeats text the agent read, naming that text. The console shows which agents actually ask Stroq, and every decision with its reason. Sessions you already had can be checked too, without installing anything. npx @stroq/cli console # the console: a private page on 127.0.0.1 npx @stroq/cli replay --all # your Claude Code sessions of the last 30 days against the policy; no hooks needed npx @stroq/cli sent # did your agent pass your keys on? key names, never values npx @stroq/cli init # hook into your agent and guard the next session A made-up machine: made-up s…",
   "sito": "https://github.com/AGGIB/Stroq",
   "fonte": "Show HN (nuovi progetti)",
   "tipo": "vetrina",
